## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem evaluates the ability to extend classic DP patterns (like the staircase problem) with an additional resource dimension, while also testing space optimization skills and recursive thinking via memoization. It distinguishes candidates who understand both bottom-up and top-down approaches, and who reason through edge cases systematically.

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra budget constraint (two-dimensional state: position + accumulated cost)
- Space optimization using rolling arrays to reduce O(n·B) space to O(B)
- Memoization on composite state (i, b) for the recursive variant
- Correct base case handling when starting from a virtual position before index 0

## Code Example
```javascript
const MOD = 1_000_000_007n;

function countWays(cost, B) {
  const n = cost.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;

  // bottom-up with rolling arrays
  let prev2 = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);
  let prev1 = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);

  // base: step 0
  if (cost[0] <= B) prev2[cost[0]] = 1;

  // base: step 1
  if (n > 1 && cost[1] <= B) prev1[cost[1]] = 1;

  // step 2 onwards
  for (let i = 2; i < n; i++) {
    const cur = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);
    for (let b = cost[i]; b <= B; b++) {
      cur[b] = (prev1[b - cost[i]] + prev2[b - cost[i]]) % MOD;
    }
    prev2 = prev1;
    prev1 = cur;
  }

  return n === 1 ? prev2[B] : prev1[B];
}

// memoized DFS variant
function countWaysDFS(cost, B) {
  const n = cost.length;
  const memo = new Map();

  function dfs(i, b) {
    if (b < 0) return 0;
    if (i < 0) return b === 0 ? 1 : 0;
    const key = `${i},${b}`;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const result = (dfs(i - 1, b - cost[i]) + dfs(i - 2, b - cost[i])) % MOD;
    memo.set(key, result);
    return result;
  }

  return dfs(n - 1, B);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests adaptive sorting algorithms, merge logic, and the ability to reason about time complexity in practical, maintainable ways.

## Key Concepts

- **Adaptive sorting**: Using a pre-check to achieve O(n) best-case performance when the array is already sorted
- **Iterative bottom-up mergesort**: Avoiding recursion by iteratively merging sorted subarrays of increasing size
- **Stability and memory usage**: Understanding the trade-offs of O(n) auxiliary space for guaranteed stability

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortWithEarlyMerge(arr) {
  // Early return if already sorted
  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] < arr[i - 1]) break;
    if (i === arr.length - 1) return arr;
  }

  // Iterative bottom-up mergesort
  const aux = [...arr];
  let width = 1;

  while (width < arr.length) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += width * 2) {
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, arr.length);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, arr.length);

      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right) {
        aux[k++] = arr[p] <= arr[q] ? arr[p++] : arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
    }

    [arr, aux] = [aux, arr];
    width *= 2;
  }

  return arr;
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis

- **Best case (O(n))**: Array is already sorted; single linear scan confirms this
- **Worst case (O(n log n))**: Array is reverse sorted; full mergesort required
- **Space complexity**: O(n) for the auxiliary buffer

## Why Asked
